
  One scope flag before anything else. "Users get notified when tasks they care about change" presupposes four things
  that don't exist in this repo: tasks with persistent identity, users, a notion of "caring about" a task
  (subscription), and a delivery channel. Notifications is the [3mlast[0m of those to build, not the first. So the first
  question isn't about notifications at all.

  [1mThe fork:[0m what substrate are we actually designing against? This determines whether the next deliverable is a
  notifications spec or a decomposition of a whole app.

  - [1mGreenfield here[0m — we design the tasks app and notifications together, and I decompose it into sub-projects (tasks +
    persistence first, then subscriptions, then delivery). Buys a coherent whole; costs more rounds before any
    notification code exists. Nothing is locked in yet, so this is the cheapest moment to choose a stack.
  - [1mReal app lives elsewhere[0m — this repo is a stub and the actual tasks system is in another codebase. Then I need to
    read that code before designing anything; a spec written against an imagined data model would be wasted work.
  - [1mFront-end-only prototype[0m — no server, no real users; notifications means in-page toasts plus a bell menu over local
    state. Much smaller, genuinely bounded-ish, but rules out cross-device/email/push later without a rewrite of the
    delivery layer.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m tell me it's greenfield if it is, and let me decompose — building a delivery pipeline on a data
  model we haven't agreed on is the expensive mistake here.
